/// returns the length (the pixel-width) of the string which that instance of
/// the structure represents. NOTE: if the Font pointer of your IntuiText
/// structure is set to NULL, you'll get the pixel-width of your text in terms
/// of the current system default font.
///
/// # Arguments
///
/// # Returns
/// Returns the pixel-width of the text specified by the IntuiText data
///
/// See also: OpenScreen()
@library("intuition.library")
extern pub fn IntuiTextLength(iText: *IntuiText) -> i32
/// Send the Workbench screen in back of all screens.
///
/// Causes the Workbench screen, if it's currently opened, to go behind all
/// other screens. This does not 'move' the screen up or down, instead only
/// affects the depth-arrangement of the screens.
///
/// # Arguments
///
/// # Returns
/// If the Workbench screen was opened, this function returns TRUE,
/// otherwise it returns FALSE.
///
/// See also: WBenchToFront(), ScreenToFront()
@library("intuition.library")
extern pub fn WBenchToBack() -> i16
/// Bring the Workbench screen in front of all screens.
///
/// Causes the Workbench Screen, if it's currently opened, to come to the
/// foreground. This does not 'move' the screen up or down, instead only affects
/// the depth-arrangement of the screen.
///
/// # Arguments
///
/// # Returns
/// If the Workbench screen was opened, this function returns TRUE,
/// otherwise it returns FALSE.
///
/// See also: WBenchToBack(), ScreenToBack()
@library("intuition.library")
extern pub fn WBenchToFront() -> i16
/// Automatically build and get response from a requester.
///
/// This procedure automatically builds a requester for you and then waits for a
/// response from the user, or for the system to satisfy your request. If the
/// response is positive, this procedure returns TRUE.
///
/// # Arguments
///
/// # Returns
/// The return value is either TRUE or FALSE. See the text above for a
/// complete description of the chain of events that might lead to either
/// of these values being returned.
///
/// See also: EasyRequestArgs(), BuildSysRequest(), SysReqHandler()
@library("intuition.library")
extern pub fn AutoRequest(window: *Window, body: *IntuiText, posText: *IntuiText, negText: *IntuiText, pFlag: u32, nFlag: u32, width: u16, height: u16) -> i16
/// Sets up a window for optimized refreshing.
///
/// This routine sets up your window for optimized refreshing. Its role is to
/// provide Intuition integrated access to the Layers library function
/// BeginUpdate().
///
/// # Arguments
///
/// # Returns
/// None
///
/// See also: EndRefresh(), layers.library/BeginUpdate(), OpenWindow() layer.library/InstallClipRegion(), layers.library/LockLayerInfo() The "Windows" chapter of the Intuition Reference Manual
@library("intuition.library")
extern pub fn BeginRefresh(window: *Window)
/// Build and display a system requester.
///
/// This procedure builds a system requester based on the supplied information.
/// If all goes well and the requester is constructed, this procedure returns a
/// pointer to the window in which the requester appears.
///
/// # Arguments
///
/// # Returns
/// If the requester was successfully created, the value returned by this
/// procedure is a pointer to the window in which the requester is
/// rendered. If the requester could not be created, this routine might
/// have called DisplayAlert() before returning (it depends on the version)
/// and will pass back TRUE if the user pressed the left mouse button and
/// FALSE if the user pressed the right mouse button. If the version of
/// Intuition doesn't call DisplayAlert(), or if it does, and there's not
/// enough memory for the alert, the value of FALSE is returned.
///
/// See also: FreeSysRequest(), DisplayAlert(), ModifyIDCMP(), exec.library/Wait(), Request()
@library("intuition.library")
extern pub fn BuildSysRequest(window: *Window, body: *IntuiText, posText: *IntuiText, negText: *IntuiText, flags: u32, width: u16, height: u16) -> *Window
/// End the optimized refresh state of the window.
///
/// This function gets you out of the special refresh state of your window. It
/// is called following a call to BeginRefresh(), which routine puts you into
/// the special refresh state.
///
/// # Arguments
///
/// # Returns
/// None
///
/// See also: BeginRefresh(), layers.library/EndUpdate(), graphics.library/UnlockLayerRom()
@library("intuition.library")
extern pub fn EndRefresh(window: *Window, complete: i32)
/// Free resources gotten by a call to BuildSysRequest().
///
/// This routine frees up all memory allocated by a successful call to the
/// BuildSysRequest() procedure. If BuildSysRequest() returned a pointer to a
/// window, then you are able to wait on the message port of that window to
/// detect an event which satisfies the requester.
///
/// # Arguments
///
/// # Returns
/// None
///
/// See also: BuildSysRequest(), AutoRequest(), CloseWindow()
@library("intuition.library")
extern pub fn FreeSysRequest(consuming window: *Window)
/// Do an Intuition-integrated MakeVPort() of a screen.
///
/// This procedure allows you to do a MakeVPort() for the viewport of your
/// custom screen in an Intuition-integrated way. This way you can do your own
/// screen manipulations without worrying about interference with Intuition's
/// usage of the same viewport.
///
/// # Arguments
///
/// # Returns
/// Starting with V39, returns zero for success, non-zero for failure.
/// Probable cause of failure is failure of graphics.library/MakeVPort().
/// Prior to V39, the return code is invalid. Do not interpret it when
/// running on pre-V39 systems!
///
/// See also: RethinkDisplay(), RemakeDisplay(), graphics.library/MakeVPort()
@library("intuition.library")
extern pub fn MakeScreen(screen: *Screen) -> i32
/// Remake the entire Intuition display.
///
/// This is the big one. This procedure remakes the entire View structure for
/// the Intuition display.
///
/// # Arguments
///
/// # Returns
/// Starting with V39, returns zero for success, non-zero for failure.
/// Probable cause of failure is failure of graphics.library/MakeVPort() or
/// of graphics.library/MrgCop(). Prior to V39, the return code is invalid.
/// Do not interpret it when running on pre-V39 systems!
///
/// See also: MakeScreen(), RethinkDisplay(), graphics.library/MakeVPort() graphics.library/MrgCop(), graphics.library/LoadView()
@library("intuition.library")
extern pub fn RemakeDisplay() -> i32
/// Grand manipulation of the entire Intuition display.
///
/// This function performs the Intuition global display reconstruction. This
/// includes rethinking about all of the ViewPorts and their relationship to one
/// another and reconstructing the entire display based on the results of this
/// rethinking.
///
/// # Arguments
///
/// # Returns
/// Starting with V39, returns zero for success, non-zero for failure.
/// Probable cause of failure is failure of graphics.library/MakeVPort() or
/// of graphics.library/MrgCop(). Prior to V39, the return code is invalid.
/// Do not interpret it when running on pre-V39 systems!
///
/// See also: RemakeDisplay(), graphics.library/MakeVPort(), graphics.library/MrgCop(), graphics.library/LoadView(), MakeScreen()
@library("intuition.library")
extern pub fn RethinkDisplay() -> i32
/// AllocMem() with tracking to make freeing easy.
///
/// This routine calls the Exec AllocMem() function for you, but also links the
/// parameters of the allocation into a master list, so that you can simply call
/// the Intuition routine FreeRemember() at a later time to deallocate all
/// allocated memory without being required to remember the details of the
/// memory you've allocated. This routine will have two primary uses: - Let's
/// say that you're doing a long series of allocations in a procedure.
///
/// # Arguments
///
/// # Returns
/// If the memory allocation is successful, this routine returns the byte
/// address of your requested memory block. Also, the node to your block
/// will be linked into the list pointed to by your RememberKey variable.
/// If the allocation fails, this routine returns NULL and the list pointed
/// to by RememberKey, if any, will be unchanged.
///
/// See also: FreeRemember(), exec.library/AllocMem()
@library("intuition.library")
extern pub fn AllocRemember(rememberKey: *Remember, size: u32, flags: u32) -> *u8
/// Free memory allocated by calls to AllocRemember().
///
/// This function frees up memory allocated by the AllocRemember() function. It
/// will either free up just the Remember structures, which supply the link
/// nodes that tie your allocations together, or it will deallocate both the
/// link nodes AND your memory buffers too.
///
/// # Arguments
///
/// # Returns
/// None
///
/// See also: AllocRemember(), exec.library/FreeMem()
@library("intuition.library")
extern pub fn FreeRemember(consuming rememberKey: *Remember, reallyForget: i16)
/// Invoke semaphore arbitration of IntuitionBase.
